---

<GuideLayout
  title="Know where the boundaries are."
  description="A durable record is useful only when its limits are clear. Read this before sharing access, scheduling work, or relying on a recovered execution."
  eyebrow="Security / Operations"
  contents={contents}
>
  <section id="trust">
    <h2>One workspace. One trust boundary.</h2>
    <p><strong>agent-workspace is a single-user, single-server-process application.</strong> Every authorized browser shares the same workspace and the local authority of that server. An access token is not a separate user account.</p>
    <div class="callout callout-warning">
      <strong>Access includes a real shell.</strong>
      <p>The terminal runs with the server process’s operating-system permissions. Provider CLIs can execute tools against the project. Do not share access with anyone you would not trust at that machine.</p>
    </div>
    <p>There is no multi-tenant isolation, per-user RBAC, billing system, or automatic Git worktree sandbox. Run with an appropriately restricted operating-system account, protect the host, and review which directories and credentials it can access.</p>
    <p>The marketing website is separate static content. Merely viewing it does not authenticate you to a workspace, open a model connection, or start a background execution.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="remote">
    <h2>Keep loopback local. Protect remote access.</h2>
    <p>The server defaults to loopback. To deliberately expose it, configure <code>AGENT_WORKSPACE_ACCESS_TOKEN</code> with a strong random value of at least 32 characters before setting <code>HOST=0.0.0.0</code> or another non-loopback address.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Use a trusted <strong>HTTPS reverse proxy</strong>. Preserve Host and Origin, support event streaming and WebSocket upgrades, and do not expose an unprotected upstream port.</li>
      <li>Users enter the token in the workspace. The server uses an HttpOnly, SameSite=Strict session cookie; the token is not stored in localStorage. Rotating the server token invalidates old sessions.</li>
      <li>Keep the token on the server. Never put it in <code>VITE_</code> / <code>PUBLIC_</code> variables, website URLs, source commits, or static build output.</li>
      <li>All token holders have the same workspace authority. Same-origin checks and a token do not replace host security or user isolation.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The desktop shell permits HTTP for loopback connections, but requires HTTPS for non-local remote servers. A remote server executes on its own host; selecting remote mode does not move your local project there.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="execution">
    <h2>Persistence is not uninterrupted execution.</h2>
    <p>Once accepted, requests, snapshots, events, and results are recorded server-side. Refreshing, navigating away, or closing a browser tab stops the subscription—not the accepted run. Reconnect to read the stored state.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Server restart:</strong> unfinished executions become interrupted. Confirmed output remains. A continuation is a new run; the old outcome is not rewritten as success.</li>
      <li><strong>Cancellation:</strong> the explicit Stop action requests cancellation. It cannot undo file changes, external tool effects, or charges that have already occurred.</li>
      <li><strong>Continuation:</strong> completed workflow step outputs can be reused, but an unfinished CLI step may already have changed the world. Inspect files and Git before approving an unsafe replay.</li>
      <li><strong>Concurrency:</strong> chat and workflows share a per-thread execution lock. Different threads can run concurrently and may still touch the same working directory.</li>
      <li><strong>External effects:</strong> the application does not promise exactly-once execution of external side effects.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Missing credentials, a provider outage, or an incompatible native session is an error to inspect—not permission to silently swap accounts or replay a request. Provider-native behavior depends on the installed compatible CLI, account, and session boundary.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="context">
    <h2>A conversation branch is not a file sandbox.</h2>
    <p>Fork preserves the parent thread and recorded run. Where compatible native provider boundaries exist, the application can use native fork behavior; otherwise it uses a bounded context handoff or reports that the operation is unsupported.</p>
    <p><strong>Branches share the project working directory by default.</strong> They do not automatically create Git worktrees, restore files, or isolate simultaneous edits. Arrange separate working directories yourself if the work needs that isolation.</p>
    <p>Merge back is a <strong>previewed, explicitly confirmed context transfer</strong> to the direct parent thread. It is not a Git merge or cherry-pick. The parent consumes it once on the next explicitly requested execution; transferring context does not automatically start that execution.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="background">
    <h2>Background means “while the server is running.”</h2>
    <p>Schedules, quota recovery, and PR polling depend on a live server process and a working provider or GitHub connection. They cannot execute while the computer is off or the service is stopped.</p>
    <dl class="definition-list">
      <div><dt>Schedules</dt><dd>Use the server timezone. Missed occurrences follow skip or coalesce policy rather than replaying the whole downtime backlog. A dispatch receipt means queued, not succeeded.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Quota recovery</dt><dd>Requires explicit authorization, an eligible limit classification, and a valid provider reset time. Unknown reset times are not guessed. Uncertain CLI side effects can require manual continuation.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>PR Watch</dt><dd>Requires the server’s GitHub credentials or <code>gh</code> login and network access. It establishes a baseline, deduplicates changes, and queues work. PR text is untrusted external input, not additional tool authority or automatic merge approval.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Desktop lifecycle</dt><dd>Closing the workspace window and quitting the application are different. The desktop host can keep its local server running after the workspace window closes; quitting stops that managed server. See the <a href="/desktop/#lifecycle">desktop lifecycle guide</a>.</dd></div>
    </dl>
    <p>No daemon installation or automatic updater is included. If always-on operation matters, operate the server under your own process supervisor and monitor the actual run outcomes.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="data">
    <h2>Protect the data directory as a secret.</h2>
    <p><code>AGENT_WORKSPACE_DATA_DIR</code> selects persistent Agent data; v3 defaults to this checkout’s <code>.local/agent-data</code>, never the old global directory. It contains <code>workspace.sqlite</code> and Provider state. Explicit CLI homes must be v3-owned. Browser preferences are not a backup of this data.</p>
    <ol class="instruction-list">
      <li>Stop the owning server before backing up or moving its data.</li>
      <li>Copy the complete data directory and any separate CLI homes to storage protected as carefully as the original credentials.</li>
      <li>Keep directory ownership and permissions restrictive. Do not assume database or credential files are encrypted at rest.</li>
      <li>Restart one server owner with the same explicit data and project paths. Do not point two processes at the same data directory.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Credentials and configured environment values are held on the server, not returned as full values to the Web settings interface. This does not remove your responsibility to protect the filesystem, logs, shell sessions, and host backups.</p>
    <p>Resource revisions detect stale saves and preserve drafts for explicit conflict handling. Legacy browser-data import is not cross-device synchronization or a promise of compatibility with archived applications.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="inspectors">
    <h2>Inspectors have different authority.</h2>
    <dl class="definition-list">
      <div><dt>Files and Git</dt><dd>Read-only inspection of the configured project root. Text previews, directory trees, and diffs have size limits and may be truncated. Reading a diff does not apply it.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Terminal</dt><dd>A real local PTY per WebSocket connection, not a durable background job or a sandbox. Disconnecting the terminal socket ends that PTY; this is separate from the durable model execution queue.</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Browser preview</dt><dd>A client-side iframe, not a server proxy. A site can refuse framing through CSP or X-Frame-Options. Same-origin privileges are disabled by default; only enable them for trusted local content.</dd></div>
    </dl>
    <p>Understand the execution boundary first, then <a href="/get-started/">set up your workspace</a>. No interface status, animation, or successful dispatch can substitute for checking the server’s final result.</p>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>
